In Spring Boot integration testing, managing the boundaries of your application context often requires replacing or wrapping real collaborators with mock implementations. This is where @MockBean and @SpyBean come into play. While standard Mockito annotations like @Mock and @Spy create standalone mock objects devoid of Spring awareness, @MockBean and @SpyBean integrate directly with the Spring TestContext framework. 

@MockBean is used to add mock objects to the Spring ApplicationContext. When you annotate a field with @MockBean, Spring looks for an existing bean of that type in the context. If it finds one, it replaces that bean with a Mockito mock. If no bean exists, a new mock is automatically defined and registered. This allows developers to isolate the component under test by stubbing out external services, database repositories, or messaging clients without spinning up real infrastructure. 

Conversely, @SpyBean wraps an existing real Spring bean in a Mockito spy rather than replacing it entirely. By default, calling methods on a @SpyBean invokes the real implementation inside the Spring container, allowing state to mutate and actual business logic to run. However, you retain the ability to selectively stub specific methods using Mockito.when(...).then() or doReturn(...).when(...), making it indispensable for legacy codebases or partial integration tests where most of a service should run natively while a specific external dependency or side effect is intercepted.

@SpringBootTest
class OrderServiceIntegrationTest {

    @Autowired
    private OrderService orderService;

    @MockBean
    private PaymentGatewayClient paymentGatewayClient;

    @SpyBean
    private InventoryService inventoryService;

    @Test
    void shouldProcessOrderSuccessfully() {
        // Stubbing the purely mocked external dependency
        Mockito.when(paymentGatewayClient.charge(Mockito.any()))
               .thenReturn(new PaymentResult(true, "TXN-123"));

        // Using doReturn to prevent real execution on a specific spy method
        Mockito.doReturn(true)
               .when(inventoryService)
               .isItemInStock(Mockito.anyString());

        OrderResponse response = orderService.placeOrder(new OrderRequest("item-1", 2));

        assertNotNull(response);
        assertTrue(response.isSuccess());
    }
}
